- op: discuss  # 38a4
  at: '2026-08-24T21:35:43Z'  # 38a4
  by: agent  # 38a4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 38a4
  clock: 3  # 38a4
  nonce: 38a4  # 38a4
  message: "THIS ITEM'S OWN PREMISE IS REFUTED. Correcting it before it misleads anyone,\nincluding me.\n\nWHAT I FILED: \"only parts[0] (language) and parts[-1] (kind) are colon-free by\nthe grammar\", therefore ~15 positional slot reads are defects, therefore a\nshrink-only gate. THE PREMISE IS FALSE.\n\nADR-0036 Ruling 1 makes the NAME slot colon-free BY RULE.\nanalyze.base.sanitize_id_name_segment folds ':' -> '.' and make_symbol_id\napplies it to EVERY name slot (WI-sikar). So parts[-2] is CORRECT on a\nwell-formed id, and the name-slot reads across the tree are the grammar being\nrelied on, not defects. Only the PATH slot is colon-tolerant (D1a).\n\nWHAT REFUTED IT: the suite, not review.\ntest_spec_validator_smoke.py::test_id_format_validator_names_the_name_slot_colon_not_the_span\nfailed against my change. That test exists because INV-dulah paid a real\ninvestigation to separate \"colon in the name slot\" from \"malformed span\".\n\nAND ONE PART OF MY CHANGE WAS AN ACTIVE REGRESSION. Span-anchoring\nvalidate_symbol_id_format made it locate the span CORRECTLY ON A MALFORMED ID,\nso an id with a colon in its name parsed clean and colon_in_name_slot fell\nsilent. A validator going quiet is the false-clean direction. Reverted; both\nvalidators keep parts[-3] deliberately, now with a comment saying why.\n\nTHE REAL ROOT CAUSE IS A PRODUCER DEFECT AT ONE LINE. analyze/base.py:880:\n\n    dst_id = f\"{lang}:{module_hint}:0-0:{callee_name}:unresolved\"\n\nmake_unresolved_edge hand-rolls the id with an f-string and never sanitises\ncallee_name -- which is exactly what spec_validator's own error message warns\nagainst (\"Use make_symbol_id(...) rather than constructing IDs with\nf-strings\"). An objc selector lands in the name slot verbatim, violating\nRuling 1.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE STANDS (Mantle, 31 .m files) -- the damage is real even\nthough my diagnosis of its layer was wrong:\n    external dsts                       319 (94 distinct)\n    dsts whose parsed name is EMPTY     268   (84%)\n    distinct names, symbol_name_slot     80\n    distinct names, parts[-2]            17   (79% of identity LOST)\n    one node objc:external:0-0::external_symbol absorbs 49 edges /\n      134 distinct call sites\n    id_format violations                 71\n\nTHE FORK -- OWNER CALL, NOT MINE. Sanitising at the producer restores the\ngrammar and reproduces all 80 distinct names (verified). BUT 107 of the 128\nprimitives in io_primitives/objc.yaml carry a colon in their name\n(fileExistsAtPath:, writeToFile:atomically:), so sanitising the edge to\nwriteToFile.atomically. BREAKS CATALOGUE MATCHING unless the qualified index\nregisters the folded form too.\n\n  ARM A  Restore the grammar at the producer (sanitise in\n         make_unresolved_edge) + register the sanitised form in the catalogue\n         qualified index. Keeps ADR-0036 Ruling 1 intact. Changes objc ids and\n         every affected stable_id. Also fixes the dedupe collapse for free.\n  ARM B  Amend ADR-0036 to admit a colon in the name slot for languages whose\n         callee names carry one, and narrow colon_in_name_slot to the maven\n         coordinate case INV-dulah actually cares about. No id churn; the\n         grammar gets more complex and every positional name read becomes a\n         latent defect (which is what I wrongly assumed was already true).\n\nI am NOT choosing. Both need an ADR edit.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED INSTEAD (reduced PR): the PATH-slot conversions, which were never\nin doubt, plus the name-slot READS behind the disclosure a reader sees -- the\ncaveat had been naming `writeToFile` for `writeToFile:atomically:`, a method\nthat does not exist. That is right under EITHER arm. No gate, no baseline, no\nvalidator change.\n\nSTILL NOT MEASURED: the linker-recovery loss (method_call_recovery / napi /\npyffi / lua_ffi each take parts[-2] off an :unresolved dst); whether any\nlanguage other than objc emits a colon-bearing callee name; whether any\nshipped verdict depends on a collapsed node."  # 38a4
- op: discuss  # a683
  at: '2026-08-24T22:34:13Z'  # a683
  by: agent  # a683
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a683
  clock: 4  # a683
  nonce: a683  # a683
  message: "BEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE FOR THE FORK 2026-08-24, from re-running WI-nakut's objc\nrepro on merged dev (@dfe3ba8b3e). Until now this item's damage was measured\nstructurally (80 selectors -> 17, 268/319 dsts nameless, 71 id_format\nviolations). It now has a USER-FACING symptom on the shipped CLI, which is the\nkind of evidence the Closure-Evidence Discipline asks for.\n\n  ! [NO-FSWRITE] Verdict: confirmed_with_caveats\n    CAVEAT (unknown_receiver_scope): ... At 1 of 1 method call site(s) the\n    receiver's type could not be determined ... distinct method(s): .\n\nThe reader is told something went unexamined and cannot be told WHAT. Repro is\n14 lines of Objective-C: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/nakut_closure_08242026/\n\nCONFIRMS THIS ITEM'S FILED root_cause EXACTLY, end to end on merged dev:\n\n  analyzer emits           objc:external:0-0:writeToFile:atomically::unresolved\n  _parse_dangling_id name  ''                        <- hand-rolled parts[-2]\n  symbol_name_slot would   'writeToFile:atomically:'\n  _dedupe_key              ('objc', 'external', '')\n  survey carries           objc:external:0-0::external_symbol\n\nWHAT THIS ADDS TO THE FORK. I had assumed the reduced PR #511 discharged\nWI-nakut's objc half. It did not, and the reason sharpens the choice: the READ\nin unknown_receiver_scope is already correct, so no further read-side conversion\ncan help. The name is destroyed at the REMAP, one site, ir._parse_dangling_id\n(ir.py:1555). Converting that one site recovers the selector and keeps entities\ndistinct in _dedupe_key -- but the RE-MINTED boundary node id then carries a raw\ncolon in its NAME slot, which ADR-0036 Ruling 1 forbids. So the fork is not\navoidable by picking a smaller fix; the smallest possible fix is already on the\nfar side of it.\n\n  ARM A  sanitise at the producer + register the folded form in the catalogue\n         qualified index (107 of 128 objc primitives carry a colon in their\n         NAME, so sanitising alone breaks catalogue matching)\n  ARM B  amend ADR-0036 to admit a colon in the name slot\n\nBoth need an ADR edit. STILL NOT DECIDING ALONE -- this is an owner call."  # a683
- op: discuss  # 184b
  at: '2026-08-25T02:47:34Z'  # 184b
  by: agent  # 184b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 184b
  clock: 5  # 184b
  nonce: 184b  # 184b
  message: "STEP 1 OF THE FIX SHIPPED 2026-08-24 — PR #515 (3a176e4589). The LOSSLESS HOME\nnow exists and is filled. Evidence + measurements:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/divuf_fix_08242026/README.md\n\nWHAT CHANGED THE FRAMING. I had presented this item as an owner FORK needing an\nADR amendment. READING ADR-0036 PROPERLY SHOWS IT WAS ALREADY DECIDED, and my\nframing was wrong. Ruling 1 makes the id's name slot deliberately lossy and says\nwhy in as many words: \"the ID is a location-addressed key, not a fidelity\nsurface -- full-fidelity names live in Symbol.name ... Consumers that need the\nexact name MUST read Symbol.name, never re-derive it from the ID.\" The ADR's own\nid-format:F3 amendment then lists the producer-side ':'->'.' sanitisation as a\nDEFERRED FOLLOW-UP. So arm A was the standing ruling all along and arm B would\nhave required REVERSING an accepted ADR. No amendment was needed.\n\nAND THE ARM-A OBJECTION DISSOLVED. I had written that sanitising \"breaks\ncatalogue matching, because 107 of 128 objc entries carry a colon\". That only\nbit because the implementation never held up Ruling 1's other half. Measured on\nthe repro: node.name '', qualified_name None, dst_ref None -- the selector\nexisted NOWHERE in the output. The boundary node is synthesised FROM the id,\nwhich made the id the name's ONLY home, the one arrangement Ruling 1 forbids.\nTHE COLON WAS NEVER THE DEFECT; the missing home was.\n\nSHIPPED:\n  1. meta['callee_name'], registered on the edge-meta axis (ADR-0024),\n     single_writer, stamped UNCONDITIONALLY by make_unresolved_edge -- the one\n     factory all 57 unresolved-external sites in 36 analyzers route through.\n     Unconditional because WI-huzuv correctly withholds dst_ref when the module\n     is unknown (exactly the objc cell), and a name living in dst_ref sometimes\n     and meta otherwise is one fact with two homes.\n  2. create_boundary_nodes prefers that home over the lossy slot.\n  3. Both disclosure sites read it through ONE helper (they are a pair:\n     _site_method parses _call_site_label back out).\n  4. _canonical_external_id folds ':'->'.' for the ID while Symbol.name keeps\n     full fidelity.\n\nITEM 4 WAS A REGRESSION I INTRODUCED AND THE VALIDATOR CAUGHT. The first cut fed\nthe lossless name into the minted id (rust:external:0-0:Vec::with_capacity:...),\nwhich id_format rejects as double_colon_separator (INV-sadiv) -- bellman went\n1 -> 16 violations. Getting Ruling 1's lossless half right by breaking its lossy\nhalf is not getting Ruling 1 right. Shipped state: bellman at 0, which also\ncleared the one violation that PRE-DATED this work.\n\nMEASURED, three layers. Mantle, ONE survey scored BOTH WAYS (instrument\ncomparison, not a cross-run diff): distinct callee names 19 -> 82; dsts with an\nempty name 179/229 -> 0; boundary nodes with name='' -> 0. Published CLI:\n\"distinct method(s): .\" -> \"distinct method(s): writeToFile:atomically:\".\n\nA SECOND LANGUAGE, found on a repo intended only as a non-destruction control:\nparts[-2] discarded the receiver type on every Rust Type::method call, so\nbellman's 13 truncated boundary nodes became 16 qualified ones --\nbatch_normalize splits back into E::G1::batch_normalize AND\nE::G2::batch_normalize; with_capacity -> Vec::with_capacity; now -> Instant::now.\nSo this was never objc-only. objc is simply where the defect produced an EMPTY\nname rather than a merely truncated one.\n\nHONEST LIMITS: no verdict flipped on either repo -- this is identity and\ndisclosure quality, not coverage. Mantle's 82/19 is CLOSE TO BUT NOT an exact\nreproduction of this item's filed 80/17 (population drift since filing). Only\nobjc and rust were measured at population level; the producer change is central\nso it reaches all 36 analyzers, but the effect on the other 34 is UNMEASURED.\n\nSTILL OPEN, which is why this stays violated: the ORIGINAL Ruling-1 deferred\nfollow-up -- producer-side sanitisation in make_unresolved_edge itself, so\nEdge.dst stops carrying the raw selector. That is now SAFE to do, and was not\nbefore: folding first would have destroyed the name the lossless home preserves.\nSequencing was the reason this landed first."  # 184b
